{
	title: 'Web transcript that reads like the demo',
	spec: `The web transcript has the improvements the old Hal tried in its Solid demo (artifact demo.tsx, tags [F1]..[F16]) and never shipped:
- Stable rows [F1, F8]: a card keeps its DOM and its local state (open or closed) through every change that leaves its item in the transcript: a snapshot replacing every item object (reconnect), a streaming item gaining text, earlier history put above it (task bq), and redraws that leave the items alone (typing, the status line), which touch no card at all. A change costs DOM work for what changed only: a streamed delta updates one card's text, a new item adds one card, and no card is removed, added or faded in again otherwise. Whether a card is open belongs to its item, not to its place in the list: switching tabs or loading earlier history never shows a card open because another card sat in that slot. Row keys must survive every change the list sees: position works only while items only append (earlier history prepends; another tab replaces them all), and an id key must exist from the item's first streamed byte, not arrive when it is saved [F8].
- Render test: the CDP test in host/web.test.ts watches the transcript with a MutationObserver and asserts, for typing three keys, a streamed reply and loading earlier history, that the cards present before stay connected and only the new items' cards are added.
- Cards [F11, F12]: every item is a card colored like the terminal (prompt, assistant text, thinking, tool call with its result, command output, turn end). Thinking and tool cards collapse, closed by default. A click anywhere on a card toggles it, except on links and a click that ends a text selection; the header is a real <button> with aria-expanded, hidden contents are inert. Opening and closing animate the height (CSS grid 0fr to 1fr); closing is faster.
- Bottom follow [F4-F7]: a reader within 50 px of the bottom stays at the same distance from it as content grows (new items, streamed text, a card opening); one scrolled further up is left alone. Measure before the change.
- Pinned bottom: a reader at the very bottom stays there until they leave it by hand. While pinned, anything else that moves the view or grows the content (a background tab, tool output, a resize, the browser clamping scrollTop) is pulled back to the bottom. Only reader input unpins: wheel, touch, scroll keys or Tab, find in page, a press on the scrollbar, a drag that moves the view, or a link to a block. Reaching the very bottom again, or sending, pins again. The 50 px rule applies only when unpinned.
- Smooth follow [F10]: a new item fades in while the view glides (strong ease-out, about 200 ms, fade about 300 ms) to the new bottom; streamed lines jump; wheel, touch or scroll keys cancel a glide; prefers-reduced-motion jumps.
- Sending [F15] glides to the very bottom even when scrolled far up, and trackpad momentum does not cancel that glide.
- Composer [F13, F14]: one bottom panel holds a status line like the terminal's (idle, thinking, writing, running <tool>, paused, reconnecting; the terminal's words and colors; a pulsing dot while busy; aria-live polite), the textarea (grows with its content up to 40% of the height) with a Send button disabled while empty, and a line of key hints. Enter sends, Shift+Enter is a newline, nothing sends during IME composition; on a touch keyboard (pointer: coarse, which has no Shift) Enter is a newline and the Send button sends. A printable key or a paste while focus is outside any other field goes into the message box at its caret (focus moves there), as in Slack.
- Hal's cursor [F16]: a blinking block cursor in the assistant color sits inside the card that is streaming (thinking or assistant text), right after its last character, dimmed while thinking streams; with nothing streaming it sits on a line after the last item. The textarea keeps its native caret.
- Phones: the page fits the visual viewport, so the tab strip and composer stay on screen when the on-screen keyboard opens (iOS ignores interactive-widget=resizes-content; mirror visualViewport height and offsetTop into CSS, artifact old-viewport.ts). Fields are at least 16px (iOS zooms on focus into smaller ones), touch targets at least 44px; the top bar, transcript and composer keep text out of the safe area (rounded corners, notch, home indicator) with viewport-fit=cover and env(safe-area-inset-*) padding; phone layouts key on pointer: coarse, not only width, so a narrow desktop window keeps the desktop layout.
- One scroller: the transcript scrolls and nothing inside it does; an opened card grows to its full height; long tool output and code wrap. An overlay (picker, sheet) scrolls itself, never the page behind it.
Not in scope: ID links, deep links and the target outline [F2, F3, F9] need short block ids that Hal items do not have yet; the demo's debug controls (slow motion, step key, offline toggle).
Test the scroll maths (gap, whether to follow, glide target) and the status line as pure functions. Check the rest in a real browser at phone and desktop widths and say in the commit what you checked. A deliberate finger gesture on the transcript cancels even a forced send glide (only leftover wheel momentum is ignored). While the finger remains down, streamed updates do not reposition the transcript, even if the first movement is still within 50px of bottom; after release the measured gap determines following again. Keyboard-open touch scrolling must never fight the user to keep the last message visible.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'demo.tsx is self-contained and runs as is under Solid 2; its comments explain each decision, including the failures behind them (why keyed-by-id rows closed and flashed; why only scroll keys cancel a glide; why the send glide is `forced`). Its CSS uses hand-picked hex colors: take the layout and motion, but colors come from colors.ts (task 1w).',
		'keepBottom() calls flush() before measuring because Solid 2 applies DOM updates in a microtask.',
		"old-scroll.ts and old-viewport.ts are the old client's near-bottom check and mobile keyboard handling (visualViewport); reference only. old-frontend-design.md is the design guidance the old Hal used for UI work.",
		"Built as: scroll.ts (pure gap/keep/target/at plus the one glide runner; the caller's change must flush()), Chat.tsx redraw() picks the mode per app.changed(): pending grew = forced glide, items grew = glide, same items = jump, nothing transcript-side = plain redraw (so typing never cancels a glide). A jump during a glide lets the glide go on; it re-aims at the moving bottom every frame.",
		"Rows are view.rows(items): a tool result joins its call's row and completed turn ends drop out, so rows are not items: anything placed by item index must map it to a row.",
		'Setting scrollTop from a script does not cancel a glide (only wheel, touch and scroll keys do); a browser check of the 20 px follow must dispatch a wheel event after scrolling or the running glide pulls it back to 0.',
		'Default <For> passes the value, not an accessor: calling it threw inside Chat and left the login form up; only the CDP test in host/web.test.ts caught it.',
		'Bug shipped: every keypress flashed the transcript black and faded it back in. Each redraw made fresh Row objects (view.rows) and fresh view.show results; Card built its plain card inside <Show fallback={plain(s())}>, and a Show fallback re-evaluates when anything it reads changes, so every card got new DOM and replayed its 300 ms fade-in. Keyed-by-position rows alone do not keep DOM: never build DOM inside a fallback/getter that reads a value recreated per redraw; pass accessors and let bindings update. The rows memo reads only the items (a memo on items gates it). The CDP test types into the box and asserts no card is removed or added.',
		"Open gap as of 88ecccc: rows are still keyed by position (<For keyed={false}>) and a Card holds `open` in its own signal, so loading earlier history (transcript.prepend) rebinds every card to another item and the open states move with the slots, and a tab switch reuses the cards of the tab before (a card open there shows the other tab's item open). Items carry no id (common/transcript.ts); a stable key needs one that exists from the first streamed delta (the old Hal keyed by saved block id and rows closed and flashed when the id arrived [F8], so it fell back to position).",
		'A fade-in is a remount detector: an item\'s entrance animation runs when its node is created, so a card that fades again was rebuilt. The old Hal and Hal both shipped that flash; the user reports it as "flashes black". Look for new DOM before tuning CSS.',
		'Old Hal lessons, same user each time: streamed text must jump, not glide; restarting a smooth scroll on every delta made the view "tremble" (old commit 3f48583: glide only when a new row appears). The cursor belongs inside the streaming card ("I thought I made this clear", artifact old-cursor-in-card.diff: an inline span after the last paragraph, made inline with display: contents/inline on the last <p>). Typing anywhere goes to the prompt (old 4094b95; code in task zc artifacts old-PromptComposer.tsx and old-composer.ts: typingKey, redirectKey, redirectPaste, skipped while a dialog is open or another field has focus).',
		"Phone layout history (old commits fde3437, ae3213e, 5250373, f14bd32, 153d3c9): position: sticky bars inside a scrolling document detach during momentum scroll and fight the URL bar, so the page is a non-scrolling 100dvh flex column whose rows are the mount element's direct children (a wrapper div between them silently killed all scrolling once); iOS keeps the layout viewport full height when the keyboard opens, so the column is sized from visualViewport; the focused-composer phone layout is gated on (pointer: coarse) so narrow desktop windows keep one row.",
		'Without viewport-fit=cover in the viewport meta, env(safe-area-inset-*) is 0 on iOS, yet the page is still drawn under the rounded corners and home indicator: the key hints at the bottom had their last letter cut by the corner (user report, iPhone). Rows that touch an edge pad with max(normal, env(inset)), including the phone padding-inline override. Not checkable in headless Chrome; check on an iPhone.',
		"The browser tests in host/web.test.ts share one headless Chrome for the file (browser() hands out the shared page; its close() clears cookies, localStorage and the forced size and goes to about:blank; afterAll kills Chrome). A launch per test cost ~0.4 s each. A browser wait must wait for what the host really sends: a tab made by tabs.create in a fresh home runs the offline intro, so waiting for a fake provider's reply there timed out silently and cost 5 s per run.",
		'Bug fixed: typing a key at the bottom shifted the transcript up by one line. The old JS autosize set the textarea to height auto to measure it; the collapsed box grew the transcript for a moment and the browser clamped its scrollTop, which stayed clamped after the real height returned. Invariant: typing never moves the transcript. The box now sizes itself with CSS field-sizing: content (Baseline since June 2026), so no script measures it. Reproduce in headless Chrome: Shift+Enter, then a key while at the bottom.',
		'Bug fixed: typing (the box growing) or sending while new items glided in stranded the view above the bottom and following stopped. A resize canceled the running glide and restored the reading anchor, which had been captured mid-glide more than 50 px up. Invariant: a resize never cancels a glide to the bottom; reflow.watch skips its restore while scroll has a glide frame pending. Reproduce in headless Chrome: a fake stream yielding a new item every 90 ms, then Shift+Enter lines and Enter while at the bottom.',
		'Bug fixed: the view stayed a few to 1000+ px short of the bottom with no reader input (drift.ts logged 7 cases in a day: small gaps after the tab was hidden, large ones mid-turn right after tool output). Cause: the 50 px rule keeps whatever gap it finds, and the glide treats any move over 2 px as the reader taking over, so a move the browser made became the new gap. Invariant: only reader input leaves the bottom (scroll.state.pinned). Not reproduced in headless Chrome (a real tab switch kept the bottom; Page.setWebLifecycleState frozen never resumes frames). Unpinning too eagerly is safe, pulling back a reader who left is not: when unsure, unpin.',
		"Bug fixed: the pinned view still stayed 53 to 1256 px short mid-turn (drift.ts logged 7 cases). Pull-back ran only on a scroll event, a DOM mutation or the tab being shown; a card growing with no mutation (its open animation, an image, a style change) triggered none, so the view stayed short until a later change. Invariant: any card's size change re-checks a pinned view (a ResizeObserver on each card, border box). host/web.test.ts grows a card by style with reduced motion and expects the bottom two frames later; it fails on the old code.",
		'Bug fixed: Backspace that emptied the last row bounced the transcript up one line and glided it back (user screen recording, Chrome desktop). CSS field-sizing: content let the browser resize the box on its own schedule, and the pinned follow glided back after each resize. Invariant: the box changes height only when its row count changes, and typing never moves the transcript. Composer.tsx sets the height by script from a hidden copy (textarea.measure: same width, padding and text, height 0, so its scrollHeight is the wanted height); the visible box never collapses to measure. A ResizeObserver on the field re-fits when the width changes. Checked once in headless Chrome: per-frame box height and transcript gap while typing hello, Shift+Enter, a, Backspace x3, Shift+Enter x2, Backspace: heights 44/65/86 px only on row changes, gap 0 every frame.'
	],
	needs: ['1b']
